(:id "port/python/packet/int128" :state :done :evidence ("internal/codegen/python/kind_int128.go" "internal/codegen/python/python_packet_int128_test.go: TestPythonPacketInt128, TestPythonPacketInt128NegativeControl" "make/python.mk: python-packet-int128-negative-control" "make generated-current") :tested-revision "1ae42230c9127a9df17a1bd989479f04f0ce65d9" :note "2026-11-15: raw uint128 and ranged int128 on fixture schemas of their own, alone and beside the core's kinds (bool, enum, uint8, int8, an if/else branch, a type by value, a union), held to the generated Go unit over serialize.go: every case's Go packet reads to exactly the Go reader's values, writes back byte-identical, and the same assignments write the same bytes, at every int128 wire width class (int32, int64 and 128-bit ranges, the whole int128 domain, a default, a zero-bit degenerate range) and at uint128's 0, 1, 2^64 and 2^128-1. Every truncation, a trailing byte and seven forged inputs the Go reader refuses raise Refused; every out-of-range, bool, float, str and None write raises InvalidValue. mypy --strict is clean over the generated package. No C++-pinned golden uses only these kinds and the core's (every examples128 golden declares fixed), so the goldens stay on the harness's skip list under that kind until the packet layer closes. The negative control lands a copy of the generated Raw reader's uint128 one too high and the gate goes red on the Go-reference read marker. Run on Ubuntu 26.04 x86_64 with the pinned CPython 3.11.18 and 3.15.2 (make dist-python), SCHEMA_SLOW=1, and make generated-current, at this revision.")
